00:49:40,714 --> 00:49:46,714
Ross's 1978 play of the same name.

14
00:53:30,000 --> 00:53:36,000
It stars Edward Woodward as the title character, Lt.

15
00:57:19,285 --> 00:57:25,285
Harry Harbord "Breaker" Morant, along with Jack
Thompson, John Waters, and Bryan Brown.

16
01:01:08,571 --> 01:01:14,571
The film concerns the 1902 court martial of lieutenants Morant, Peter Handcock and George
Witton—one of the first war crime prosecutions in British military history.

17
01:04:57,857 --> 01:05:03,857
Australians serving in the British Army during the Second Anglo-Boer War, Morant, Handcock, and Witton stood
accused of murdering captured enemy combatants and an unarmed civilian in the Northern Transvaal.

18
01:08:47,142 --> 01:08:53,142
The film is notable for its exploration of the Nuremberg Defence, the
politics of the death penalty and the human cost of total war.

19
01:12:36,428 --> 01:12:42,428
As the trial unfolds, the events in question are shown in flashbacks.

20
01:16:25,714 --> 01:16:31,714
The film won ten 1980 Australian Film Institute Awards including: Best Film, Best Direction, Best
Screenplay, Best Leading Actor (for Jack Thompson) and Best Supporting Actor (Bryan Brown).

21
01:20:15,000 --> 01:20:21,000
It was also nominated for an Academy Award for the Best Adapted Screenplay.

22
01:24:04,285 --> 01:24:10,285
Breaker Morant remains the movie with which Beresford is most identified and has "hoisted the
images of the accused officers to the level of Australian icons and martyrs".

23
01:27:53,571 --> 01:27:59,571
In a 1999 interview Beresford explained that Breaker Morant "never pretended
for a moment" that the defendants were not guilty as charged.

24
01:31:42,857 --> 01:31:48,857
He had intended the film to explore how wartime atrocities can be
"committed by people who appear to be quite normal".

25
01:35:32,142 --> 01:35:38,142
Beresford concluded that he was "amazed" that so many people see his film as
being about "poor Australians who were framed by the Brits".

26
01:39:21,428 --> 01:39:27,428
The film explores themes of war crimes, writing,
mythology, south africa, military court.

27
01:43:10,714 --> 01:43:16,714
The tone is emotional.
